WEBVTT

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.800
Terrain changes altitude relationships and can hide the aircraft, create wind effects,

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.875
or reduce emergency landing options.

00:00:06.875 --> 00:00:09.440
Plan clearance relative to the actual surface and obstacles,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.880
not only an abstract launch elevation.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:16.000
Common hazards include trees, branches, wires, towers, antennas, buildings, cranes,

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.105
vehicles, people, and temporary equipment.

00:00:18.105 --> 00:00:22.170
Thin wires and small branches can be difficult for both pilots and obstacle sensors to

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:22.705
detect.

00:00:22.705 --> 00:00:25.840
Launch and recovery surfaces should be stable, clear, and controlled.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.155
Dust, loose debris, snow, water, slope, or vegetation can affect motors, sensors,

00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:31.800
payloads, and crew footing.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.430
Select alternate landing areas before takeoff.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.560
Structures can block sky view and distort satellite-navigation signals.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.410
Urban canyons, overhangs, cliffs,

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.501
and dense vegetation may reduce position accuracy or create reflected signals.

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:47.560
A stable position-hold display should not be treated as a permanent guarantee.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.335
Obstacle-avoidance systems have blind spots and performance limits related to size,

00:00:51.335 --> 00:00:54.137
texture, lighting, speed, direction, and sensor cleanliness.

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:57.270
They assist the operator but do not replace visual awareness, clearance,

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.040
or conservative path planning.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.960
Maintain a simple route with sufficient space and an escape direction.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.840
If people, vehicles, equipment, or conditions change, pause or end the mission.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.130
A closer view is not worth losing safe clearance.
